---
import Quickstart from "../../components/landing/Quickstart";
import { consumer, provider } from "../../data/examples";
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { releaseInfo } from "../../lib/release-info";

const isUnreleased = releaseInfo.version === "unreleased";
const displayVersion = isUnreleased ? "1.0.0-beta.1" : releaseInfo.version;
---

<Layout
  title="Quickstart — Agent Interaction Kit"
  description="Build AIK, describe your provider and consumer contracts, and catch incompatible tool changes in CI."
>
  <article class="docs shell">
    <a class="text-link" href="/">← Back to AIK</a><span class="eyebrow">QUICKSTART</span>
    <h1>Make the agreement<br /><em>explicit.</em></h1>
    <p class="docs-lead">
      Two JSON manifests describe the tool contract between your agent and your interface. AIK
      checks whether they agree.
    </p>
    <h2>{isUnreleased ? "01 / Build AIK" : "01 / Install AIK"}</h2>
    {
      isUnreleased ? (
        <p>
          Use Node.js 24 and pnpm 10.26.1 for this repository. The npm release is in preparation;
          build the CLI locally today.
        </p>
      ) : (
        <p>
          Install <code>@agent-interaction-kit/core</code> in your project with{" "}
          <code data-doc-command="docs-install">
            npm install --save-dev --save-exact @agent-interaction-kit/core@{releaseInfo.version}
          </code>
          .
        </p>
      )
    }
    <Quickstart client:load version={releaseInfo.version} />
    <h2>02 / Describe both sides</h2>
    <p>
      Save two manifests in your project: <code>aik.provider.json</code> (what the agent provides) and
      <code>aik.consumer.json</code> (what the UI expects). Below are two complete, minimal manifests
      from the verified fixtures:
    </p>
    <div class="manifest-downloads">
      <a class="button secondary" href="/examples/provider.json" download="aik.provider.json"
        >Download provider ↓</a
      ><a class="button secondary" href="/examples/consumer.json" download="aik.consumer.json"
        >Download consumer ↓</a
      >
    </div>
    <details>
      <summary>aik.provider.json · what the agent provides</summary><pre
        class="code-block"><code>{JSON.stringify(provider, null, 2)}</code></pre>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>aik.consumer.json · what the UI expects</summary><pre
        class="code-block"><code>{JSON.stringify(consumer, null, 2)}</code></pre>
    </details>
    <p>
      Downloading or saving example manifests proves your local setup and verifies that the CLI
      runs. However, protecting your real application requires adapting and exporting schemas from
      your actual backend agent tools and frontend UI components. You can use the optional <a
        class="text-link"
        href="https://github.com/DevJoaoLopes/agent-interaction-kit/blob/main/skills/generate-contracts/SKILL.md"
        >generate-contracts skill</a
      > to assist in extracting manifests from your codebase.
    </p>
    <h2>03 / Check the contract</h2>
    <p>Run the check using the installed package binary:</p>
    <pre
      class="code-block"><code data-doc-command="docs-check">npx --no-install aik check \
  --provider aik.provider.json \
  --consumer aik.consumer.json --strict</code></pre>
    <p>For ad-hoc execution without installing the package locally:</p>
    <pre
      class="code-block"><code data-doc-command="docs-dlx">{`pnpm dlx @agent-interaction-kit/core@${displayVersion} check \\\n  --provider aik.provider.json \\\n  --consumer aik.consumer.json --strict`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Rename <code>total</code> to <code>amount</code> in the provider's return schema, including its
      required list. Run again: AIK reports <code>AIK-RESULT-002</code> and exits with code 1. Restore
      the field to pass.
    </p>
    <h2>04 / Use it in CI</h2>
    <p>
      Add a contract test script to your <code>package.json</code>:
    </p>
    <pre
      class="code-block"><code data-doc-command="docs-script">{`{
  "scripts": {
    "test:contracts": "aik check --provider aik.provider.json --consumer aik.consumer.json --strict"
  }
}`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      In CI (e.g. GitHub Actions), perform a frozen dependency install and run your contract test:
    </p>
    <pre
      class="code-block"><code data-doc-command="docs-ci">- name: Install dependencies
  run: npm ci

- name: Test contracts
  run: npm run test:contracts</code></pre>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <caption>Result and exit code with --strict</caption><thead>
          <tr><th>Result</th><th>Exit</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead
        ><tbody>
          <tr><td>Pass</td><td>0</td><td>Compatible within the supported schema checks.</td></tr><tr
            ><td>Fail</td><td>1</td><td>A breaking contract change was detected.</td></tr
          ><tr
            ><td>Error</td><td>2</td><td
              >Invalid input: missing manifest files, malformed JSON, or invalid arguments.</td
            ></tr
          ><tr
            ><td>Inconclusive (with --strict)</td><td>2</td><td
              >Schema contains unsupported constructs (e.g. <code>AIK-SCHEMA-001</code>). Without <code
                >--strict</code
              >, exits 0.</td
            ></tr
          ></tbody
        >
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      <strong>Inconclusive is not a pass.</strong> Without <code>--strict</code>, an inconclusive or
      unknown report exits 0. Keep strict mode enabled in CI. For example, a return schema with <code
        >not</code
      > or <code>$ref</code> is inconclusive and emits <code>AIK-SCHEMA-001</code>, causing exit
      code 2 when <code>--strict</code> is enabled.
    </p>
    <p>
      AIK compares the manifests you supply. It does not validate live traffic, automatically repair
      code, or prove that the implementation matches its manifest. Keep contracts reviewed and
      updated alongside code.
    </p>
    <h2>05 / Diagnostic codes</h2>
    <p>
      AIK emits standardized diagnostic codes to report breaking schema drifts, missing
      capabilities, or unsupported constructs:
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <caption>Diagnostic codes emitted by aik check</caption><thead
          ><tr><th>Code</th><th>Severity</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead
        ><tbody
          ><tr
            ><td><code>AIK-TOOL-001</code></td><td>Error</td><td
              >Required tool missing in provider manifest.</td
            ></tr
          ><tr
            ><td><code>AIK-TOOL-002</code></td><td>Error</td><td
              >Execution side mismatch (e.g. backend vs frontend).</td
            ></tr
          ><tr
            ><td><code>AIK-INPUT-001</code></td><td>Error</td><td
              >Mandatory parameter requirement violation (contravariance).</td
            ></tr
          ><tr
            ><td><code>AIK-INPUT-002</code></td><td>Error</td><td
              >Parameter enum restriction mismatch (contravariance).</td
            ></tr
          ><tr
            ><td><code>AIK-RESULT-001</code></td><td>Error</td><td
              >Result root type mismatch or missing return schema.</td
            ></tr
          ><tr
            ><td><code>AIK-RESULT-002</code></td><td>Error</td><td
              >Required result field is missing from provider return schema.</td
            ></tr
          ><tr
            ><td><code>AIK-RESULT-003</code></td><td>Error</td><td
              >Required result field scalar type is incompatible.</td
            ></tr
          ><tr
            ><td><code>AIK-SCHEMA-001</code></td><td>Unknown</td><td
              >Schema contains unsupported constructs (e.g. not, $ref).</td
            ></tr
          ></tbody
        >
      </table>
    </div>
    <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/DevJoaoLopes/agent-interaction-kit"
      >Explore the source and full CLI reference ↗</a
    >
  </article>
</Layout>
